index 4838892b16b08d0f38fe2a54900895da32579afa..5412d1055f5d78c73cea4fa91b80e26bcd6c1227 100644
--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -13,4 +13,23 @@ Invalid syntax and unresolved references throw an error, including `-` and out-o
 when accessing arrays. Pass plain pointer strings, not URI fragments such as `#/items/0`.
 Resolution does not mutate the document and returns selected objects and arrays by reference.
 
+`applyPatch(document, patch)` applies an [RFC 6902](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6902.html)
+JSON Patch array, supporting `add`, `remove`, `replace`, `move`, `copy`, and `test`.
+Operations run in order and throw on invalid patches, unresolved references, or failed tests.
+The result is a new value; neither the document nor patch is mutated, even on failure.
+
+```ts
+import { applyPatch } from './src/index.ts';
+
+applyPatch({ items: ['first'] }, [
+  { op: 'add', path: '/items/-', value: 'second' },
+  { op: 'test', path: '/items/0', value: 'first' },
+]); // { items: ['first', 'second'] }
+```
+
+Paths and `from` locations use plain JSON Pointer strings. An empty path targets the root;
+removing it returns `undefined`, and a subsequent `add` at the root can create a document again.
+Array additions accept an index up to the array length or `-` to append. Other array references
+must select an existing element. `test` compares JSON values recursively, ignoring object key order.
+
 Run `npm test` with Node.js 22.18 or newer.
